INT: They became more traditional?

DT: Well, yes, more-- more modern, really, I would say. But we all very-- my husband still had gone to cheder, was very knowledgeable in Hebrew, Yiddish. He spoke Russian. He spoke Polish. Had-- had very good education, really. He was-- he was a linguist, I would say.

And I always liked-- I always went for education, I must say. All the people that I was interested in, I always went very much for brains. I must say that's true. Well, we were brought up that way, mostly. Jewish homes always went for a lot of education.

And I would say if I wouldn't have married I probably would have gone on with my education at the university in Vienna, because most of my girlfriends went on. And-- and I have many girlfriends that became physicians, because the medical school was free, almost. And--

INT: Your husband did not want you to continue your education?

DT: No. No. I-- I probably just took up many hobbies at that time, more so. And I helped him, then, in the business, in bookkeeping. I always liked, somehow-- my father, I helped him in his store with bookkeeping. I helped my husband, too.

INT: Were you living with your parents when you got married?

DT: Yes. Yes, sure. Everybody lived with their parents at that time.

INT: No, when you got married.

DT: No, no.

INT: Once you-- once you got married.

DT: No. I thought you meant before I got married, because, you know, even my brothers stayed all the time with my parents. No. No, my husband-- we had right away a beautiful apartment.

We went to Yugoslavia on the honeymoon. I'll never forget that it was such a beautiful country, to Dubrovnik, Ragusa. We went down-- it was beautiful.

Yugoslavia has such a nice-- has such nice memories. I can't believe what happened to this country, really. Beautiful country.

INT: When-- when were you aware what was happening in Germany in-- like, starting 1933?

DT: I--

INT: Were you aware what was happening there?

DT: No, not-- not at all. In Vienna-- we had a very good life in Vienna.

INT: You were 20-- 20 years old. Did you read newspaper? Listen to radio?

DT: No. I remember my-- my parents-in-law-- you know, my husband's parents used to go to Czechoslovakia to the baths, you know, Franzensbad. You know about it? Carlsbad. They used to go to the bath, the spas. And they met a lot of German Jews there.

And they always used to say, well, we still are living here. I'm sure Hitler will not be successful. And it's not so bad yet. They always used to say that.

But there were some people that came to Vienna. And I remember my husband was even interested in them. That I remember, that he came there to collect money and to go to Israel. A lot of people from Germany had come to Vienna to get away from Germany.

And my husband even was very interested to help some of those people. I remember that exactly. We went to meetings, and they spoke about it. But nobody-- nobody ever was thinking that it would happen in Vienna, I don't--

INT: It did not frighten you.

DT: No. No, it didn't frighten us until we heard that Hitler was marching into-- to Austria, really.

INT: When was that?

DT: That was in March '38. And I had-- my daughter was born in '36, right?

INT: Which daughter is that?

DT: Edith. My daughter Edith was born in '36. She was a beautiful child, and very smart. She is still very smart.

And we had a beautiful apartment. My furniture were all brand new because we had the carpenters from the business. And everything was-- was made to order, really.

And my dowry, everything was done with hand embroidery. And really, I mean-- you know, you had to have a dowry. And you had to have a [NON-ENGLISH] too. You know what a [NON-ENGLISH] is.

INT: You said you had a good life.

DT: But I had a good--

INT: You-- you had a good life.

DT: Yeah, I had a good life. I-- I-- my parents provided well for me. And my husband always provided well for me. So I--

INT: What happened when Hitler invaded Austria?

DT: When Hitler-- well, my husband had then another store. He opened up another store in another district on his own name. The other store was in his father's name.

And my son was born-- when Hitler marched in in March, I was expecting another child. I was pregnant. And my son was born, then, in-- when I heard that Hitler's marching in, I said to my husband-- I remember the night. We were listening to the radio. I said, Emil, please let's go to Pressburg, which is Czechoslovakia, which is right on the border next to Vienna, an hour by train.

And I said, Emil, let's go. Let's run. Really, I was frightened.

And, of course, it-- it-- we didn't do it. I was pregnant. And I had one little child. And-- and, you know, you were established. We had-- I had-- we had a apartment building in Vienna. We had two stores. And I lived quite middle class, well.

INT: And you say your son was born?

DT: My son was born August the 2nd.

INT: What's his name?

DT: George. George Albert. At that time we named him already after the English king, because we wanted to have an English name.

And as soon as Hitler came in, everybody was running to the embassies, you know, to-- to get visas. We were running to the American embassy. And all kinds-- people tried to get to Cuba and to South America and--

INT: Now, when you say we were running, who--

DT: Run-- running because so many people-- no, no. In Vienna, to the embassies.

INT: I-- I know. And then we said, 'we.' Who is we? You and--

DT: Well, my--

INT: --and your husband and your-- and your parents?

DT: To going to-- yes. Everybody was going to try to get out. But that was already before the Kristallnacht. Sure, before the Kristallnacht. Yeah. That was right when Hitler came in. There was right among the-- and the stores were taken over right away.

INT: Right away?

DT: Well, after a while the stores were taken. Everything went-- it was almost organized, but it was a lot of illegal stuff going on.

INT: Tell me about the laws that the-- the Germans--

DT: The laws?

INT: Yeah, the Germans imposed on the--

DT: Well-- well--

INT: --for the Jews, and when.

DT: Well, right away-- after a while, the stores were taken over. They came into the store with the uniforms, the SS or Gestapo, whatever it was. And they said, you have to get out of the stores. It's going to be taken over by us. So my husband-- by the German Gestapo.

So my husband already stayed home. But it was all done almost legally. The money was put on a [NON-ENGLISH], which means a-- a closed bank account. And they gave us money to live on. It was almost very German, legally done by-- by the Gestapo.

But there were a lot of illegal dones by the Viennese-- illegal Nazis. And they did a lot of horrible things to the people. They-- they accosted them in the streets. They had to wash the floors-- the streets. And--

INT: They made the Jews do those things.

DT: They made the Jews do that.

INT: What do you mean by illegal Nazis?

DT: Well, that was-- they were just hoodlums, probably, that took that on. You know, that was not done by the Germans. It was done by a lot of Austrians, these illegal things.

INT: Who joined the-- the Nazi party?

DT: Who joined the Nazi party. But you see, I must say the German Nazis were very well organized. You know, everything was done by law, even the killing.

INT: Did you go and register as-- as a Jew?

DT: Yes. I got this-- this document. You know, we had to fingerprint. And everybody had-- when I get a passport, they put on-- on everybody's name they put on my-- my name, first name, and Sarah. And my daughter's name was Edith and Sarah. And George's name was George and Israel. And that was put into the passport right away.

INT: Was there anything saying Jewish, or Juden?

DT: Oh, yes, sure. They put right away a stamp on my passport. And they gave me identification that I am a Jewess. And--

INT: Were there any curfews?

DT: Pardon?

INT: Were there any curfews imposed by the Nazis?

DT: Curfews? Well, we had-- yes. It-- it started with the food. I think the food became restricted too. We had to have-- what do you call that?

INT: Rationing?

DT: Cards. Ration--

INT: Cards.

DT: Ration cards.

INT: What I mean by-- by-- by-- by curfews--

DT: Ration. Ration.

INT: --is--

DT: Curfews to go out in the street?

INT: Yes.

DT: I don't really remember that. Really, I must say I don't remember that at all. I-- I-- I remember I was walking in the street and I was pregnant, and a young little boy with a uniform came to me. And he wanted to take me somewhere to wash windows or-- or-- or-- or clean tanks. They had dirty tanks.

INT: How did he know you were Jewish?

DT: He asked. He asked me on the street. He asked me, are you Jewish? And I said, yes. And then he said, come along with me to do some of that work.

And I said, no, I'm pregnant. And I had really a card from a doctor. And he let me go. So that was--

INT: You say a younger boy. How-- how old was he?

DT: Maybe 18. But he had already that-- that khaki uniform that they wore, and yeah. They sent them out to do these things, the youngsters. And then it-- it was the stores. You know, they wrote 'Jew' on the stores. And they-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] I think most of the things happened, really, after Kristallnacht, which was really the-- the most horrible things.

INT: What happened then?

DT: Well, my-- my husband was home at that time. We were home. George was born August the 2nd. And I had registered in--

INT: 1938.

DT: '38. And I had registered-- I have the documents here. I had registered in a hospital which was run by-- I-- I don't know who it was. It was called Rudolfinerheim. I don't know what it was run by.

And at the end of July I got a notification by the German Red Cross that I cannot come to-- to give birth there if I'm-- if I'm Jewish. I cannot come there. And they canceled my registration there. I have the documents still, the original documents.

INT: So where did you go?

DT: So I went to a private-- they used to call it sanatorium. And I was surprised that I could get in so quickly, because George was born, then, a few days after that. And I had to have the circumcision in-- in my home. Couldn't have it done anywhere. I know some friends that those children were born at the same time never had any circumcision. I remember I had a physician, even, who was allowed to be a mohel, who circumcised George.

And I even had, at that time, still non-Jewish help in my home, you see, that-- when George was born in August. And they were very nice, I must say. They were so helpful, you know.

It was always easy to have help in Vienna because the girls came from the countries, from the farms. And they were happy to be in Vienna. And they had a good home always in the Jewish homes. And they were very devoted. I had no--

INT: Did they turn around? Did they change their mind--

DT: No. No, the girls--

INT: Once the-- once the Nazis occupied?

DT: No. I cannot-- I did not have any of these experiences in my home of any of the girls. We treated them well, and they were nice.

INT: What about neighbors who were not Jewish?

DT: Neighbors-- well, I'll tell you what happened in the Kristallnacht with-- with our janitor, whatever.

INT: Yes, tell me.

DT: Yeah. Well, I just want to tell you that my husband, in his business, had one young man-- he had a few young boys working for them, young men. And one of them, Fritz-- one of them. He had, right away when Hitler came, also put out the swastika.

And the other one, Fritz-- I have a picture of him. Sent us a card with such a nice inscription. My best friend, our loving man, Emil. He was so devoted. He came to my house and brought some things for the children after my husband went to Dachau. He was a very devoted young man-- non-Jewish.

INT: You just mentioning the name Dachau. Why did he go to Dachau?

DT: Well, in the Kristallnacht they-- that was--

INT: Which was what-- what--

DT: That was--

INT: When was Kristallnacht?

DT: November 10.

INT: 1938.

DT: 1938.

INT: Eight.

DT: It was when my son was a few month old. We had-- we were the only Jewish people in that house. I think there was another person that was a woman with a sister that lived there that was Jewish.

But we were the only family in this house. There were mostly non-Jewish people. And never had any bad experience with anyone, I must say, not-- not whatsoever.

So-- but the janitor, the concierge was married to a-- her husband was a policeman at-- at the Viennese police. And they had three sons. And when the Kristallnacht was-- they came to our apartment. I-- I think it was him, or maybe just another policeman.

And they knocked at the door. And I must say, I keep on saying that maybe I shouldn't have opened the door. And they came in. And they said to Emil, you come along with us. Pack some underwear or something and come along with us. And he--

INT: Did he ask why?

DT: I-- I just-- it-- it's-- you know, these things are not very clear to me sometimes. And he just was taken out. And then we heard that it happened to so many people, because they picked them up in the streets. They picked them up anywhere. They went to apartments.

INT: Did they do in Vienna what they did in-- in all the German cities during Kristallnacht? Did they--

DT: They were all over.

INT: --fire the-- the synagogues and--

DT: The Kristallnacht-- all the synagogues were burning. Then we heard--

INT: In Vienna also.

DT: In-- oh, all the synagogues. Only one synagogue-- and that's where I married. And the synagogue is still there now. And it was even lately written up a lot, the synagogue.

It was like two houses. And you had to go through-- through one house. And in the back of it was the synagogue. A beautiful, very old-- they even had a stamp issued for its 150th anniversary. I have that stamp.

INT: Now, your husband was arrested? And then--

DT: And my husband was taken-- we didn't know where to. We just didn't know where to. And from that moment on, it really was hell on Earth. When we found out that so many-- it was like the whole city, all the stores were-- were burning, and-- and the synagogues were burning, and the people were arrested.

And then, next few days, everybody that had somebody missing went to the police. And then we found out where they were kept. They were kept in some armories, you know. We had a lot of big armories in Vienna. They were kept there.

And they were-- they-- nobody knew exactly where they were. And we used to go there every day, where we found out they were imprisoned there. And I remember the SS men standing there with the uniforms. I'll never forget those pictures. And all these Jewish wives looking for their husbands.

INT: How did they treat those Jewish wives who were looking for their husbands?

DT: Pardon?

INT: How were they treating you as a Jewish wife looking for their husband?

DT: They were very cynical. Very cynical. They-- they always-- they're very polite in a way. You know, very polite. They had such a funny way. Always the Gestapo always acted that way, very-- very polite and-- and not-- but cynical, like they would put in some words, you know.

INT: Were they physical? Did they do anything physical?

DT: Physical? I wouldn't say-- not when we were looking for our husbands. But we knew that they did a lot of things to these people that were imprisoned, because I found out from my husband. And I found out from some people that came out that were not taken to Dachau then what they did. And you want to, I can tell you what they told me what they did.

INT: Well, tell me what-- what-- what your husband-- what-- what happened to him. He went to Dachau?

DT: Well, until I found out that he was in Dachau, I got this card that you-- that I showed you from him. And he was-- he was tortured before, a lot, very much.

INT: Where? Where was he tortured?

DT: In the prisons in Vienna. They-- they-- they beat them. They-- they-- they pulled out the hair from the people's beards, you know, when older-- people with beards came there. They-- they really were tortured.

INT: Did your husband tell you why he was tortured?

DT: Oh, well, just because he was a Jew. You know, you Jew--

INT: They tried to get information out of him, or what?

DT: Oh, they always used to say, you-- you Jew, where do you have your money? And then-- you know, they used to say terrible things to them, really, always. I mean, it's-- it's-- it's hard to describe it. But, you know--

INT: Were you aware there was a camp called Dachau before-- before your husband was sent there?

DT: I don't know, really. I really don't know. I-- I-- see, like I say, there are certain things I-- I'm ignorant about. I know that they had imprisoned in Germany a lot of socialists, right? Communists. But I don't know if it was-- the name Dachau was familiar to me. I really don't know that.

INT: How long did he stay there?

DT: Well, he-- like when-- when he was there, he wrote me in this card that I should-- I finally got the card till I found-- I-- I had nursed George, really, all the time. And then I had to stop nursing, right, because I-- I was very distraught. And I was looking to find out where he is constantly.

And-- and I really got quite sick at that time. And-- and even my son got very sick, I remember. He was a baby. And the doctor-- I took him to the doctor once.

And he said to me-- she. There was a woman. She was half Jewish. She said, don't you want to have the child, she said to me? And I-- I-- I mean, of course I wanted the child very much.

So I-- I only know when she said that to me that he was really-- he-- he suffered. He was three month old. And he had suffered a lot.

And when my husband was taken away, I was out in the street one day. And I wanted to go back to my apartment. And I come upstairs-- we had an elevator in the house. I come upstairs. And the apartment was sealed by the Gestapo. And I couldn't get into the apartment.

And I had both my little children. George was three month, and Edith was not-- was two years old. I had them in-- in-- in a carriage. And I had no diapers, no clothing.

And I ran to the police there. And I'll never forget that, too. I ran to the police station, which was very far away. But probably with both kids. And thought-- and spoke to a policeman there, and want I to get into my apartment. I was banging with my hands on the chest of the policeman-- I got all numb. I really remember that all the time-- to get me into my apartment. But he didn't let me get into my apartment because they said we have-- the people that were taken to Dachau-- taken prisoner on the Kristallnacht, they might have weapons in their homes-- the Jewish people have weapons in their homes.

So I ran to my-- ran again. It was so far away with a carriage-- to my sister-in-law, who lived quite far away. And I stayed there. And I don't know exactly how I could get into my apartment again. But I will tell you about that later on more. I just tell you that when I found out that my husband was in Dachau and he sent me that card, I should go--

INT: Do you remember what he said in the card?

DT: Yeah. He said in the card he is all right. You know, he wanted to-- he wanted to--

INT: Reassure you?

DT: Reassure me. And he said I should try to get him some papers to leave the country, because he had to promise to leave the country. In the meantime, we were registered at the American consulate to emigrate to the United States. But we were Polish quota, because then we became Polish quota. And it was a very, very difficult--

INT: Even though you were-- your husband was an Austrian citizen?

DT: We were-- yeah, sure. Sure. We became Polish quota. The-- that's what the American-- well, that's another story. That's what the American immigration law was, that you had to go back to your birthplace and-- which was, at the time, Poland-- at the time that we wanted the-- the visa.

So I-- but we had registered. I-- and we had written to the United States because my father had a brother and a sister there, and a lot of cousins. And my mother had some-- an aunt and a lot of children and cousins.

We had quite a lot of family in the United States which had emigrated after the First World War to the United States. In fact, my father was supposed to go after the First World War also to the United States because he had already family there. But he didn't want to. We-- we stayed in Vienna.

So we were registered and waiting for our visa. But when Emil was in Dachau, I knew that we will not get the visa so soon. So I tried to get some visas from any South American embassy, whatever it was, even the ship's company. They sold visas, which probably were not even legal. But I bought him a visa to Paraguay.

INT: We're going to stop right now and change the tape. And we'll go right back to the visa and Paraguay.

DT: Yeah. Yeah. That got him out. That got him out.INT: Tape three, interviewing [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Dora Tanner. You were just-- your, your husband got you, you said, to go to the American, South American Embassy, and got a visa. And you got a visa?

DT: Yeah. I got a visa. It wasn't even the embassy. It must have been some of the ship's companies, or whatever. There was so much illegal stuff going on. And I bought this visa.

INT: Just for your husband?

DT: For my husband, yes. And, of course, you had to buy always a ship's ticket, too. You know, that was the deal. So then I went to the Gestapo with that. And again, I will not remember exactly how it was, but I went to the Gestapo, showed that, and he was released after, after-- he was taken in there in November and he came out in January. So it was really, in comparison, a short time, but there's so much happening.

INT: When he was released, did he tell you the kind of life that he had in, in Dachau?

DT: Yes, he did tell me.

INT: What did he tell you?

DT: He, he-- first, when he came home, he, he came to my sister-in-law because that's where I lived. I didn't live in my apartment anymore. But I probably corresponded to, with him and told him where I am now.

He came in. It was a surprise. I didn't know exactly when he would come probably. His head was shaven. He had lost a lot of weight. He had a terrible look in his eyes. And like I always say, he was such a good father and husband, but he didn't even care so much to look at the children or find out about the children. All he said, I have to get out of this country. I have to get out. I, I had promised that I would leave this country. That's how they let me get out of Dachau. But he--

INT: Did he tell you what kind of life he had in Dachau?

DT: They were tortured. They were taken out. It was wintertime, you know, when he was there. They were taken out in their pajamas and they poured water over them and the water froze on them. There were no toilets. They had to get to the toilet in the middle of the night just to get in line, you know, to be able to go to the toilet. I think they just got some piece of bread and, and some dirty soup or something. He didn't--

INT: Did he tell you there were only Jews there or was it a mixed people?

DT: Well, there were mixed people, and he told me about one German non-Jew, who was a socialist or maybe a communist. There is a difference. And he gave my husband a lot of advice, how to behave, how to survive there. He had been there for a long time.

And he gave my husband the address of his wife and his family, and my husband promised him that when he gets out, that he will give them, send them some money and tell them about him. And he really did that. I don't know the name of him, but I know that he did that. He was nice to him, this man. And--

INT: So what happened when he came home?

DT: When he came home, like I told you, he was like a changed man. He was, he was frightened, you know. You can see he was very frightened and, and very-- he had lost a lot of weight. They knocked out his teeth. He-- all he wanted to is get out, get out. And he had to go every day, as long as he was in Vienna still, to the Gestapo to be there, you know, to anmeldung.

He had to be there every day at the Gestapo. And whenever he came there, he said they were holding a gun and he was against the wall. And they said to him, where do you have your money, Jew, you Jew, you dirty Jew. You know, just torturing constantly. And when are you going to get out of this country?

And as long as he couldn't get out, he had to go there every time. But we had-- this girl who worked for my brother-in-law, a Jewish girl, her parents had a man that came there to stay with him. He traveled constantly between Holland and, and Vienna. And he had some-- he helped people to get out by providing false passports.

And he-- I must have gotten in touch with him through that girl that worked for her, from, from my brother-in-law, because the parents gave him a room to rent there, this man. And he got, for my husband, a Dutch passport with my husband's picture in it, which was a forgery, really, but he had probably connections to save the Jews to get out, and be paid for that, of course. And my-- as soon as my husband got that, he left Vienna with that passport.

INT: By himself?

DT: By himself, to Antwerp, Belgium. And when he arrived in-- he said to me in the store-- he wrote it to me later on-- when he was in the train, there were Nazis and Gestapo sitting next to him, Nazis. And they were having fun and they offered him something to eat. They didn't realize that a Jew would be-- although he had shaven hair.

And he was very frightened on the train, but he finally went over the border. When he came over the border-- there was always some kind of signals that they gave you, a ticket, and you had the other half of the ticket. It's like in a movie almost.

And he gave the man who, who got him there, who waited for him there in Antwerp, he gave him back the passport. And he stayed in Antwerp for over a year, my husband, and I stayed in Vienna. He was-- had to get out of Vienna, you know. He had-- otherwise the Gestapo would have taken him again.

INT: But it didn't bother you? That's why you stayed in Vienna?

DT: When I stayed in Vienna, there was such an incident. My husband had sent some mail. Before he left, he had sent some mail. My brother had been already in Antwerp, illegal.

INT: Which brother was that?

DT: My younger brother, Morris. He had gone illegally over the border. He had gone through a lot, too, until he finally ended up also in Antwerp. In Antwerp, a lot of Jewish-Viennese refugees were there, and they were helped by the Jewish community. My husband gave English lessons, and he really supported himself, himself there. He was a very hardworking person.

So my husband had sent something, some mail, to, to, to my brother's address. And one day-- I stayed in my sister-in-law's house-- a man comes up, not in uniform. But he was from Germany, and he worked probably for the Gestapo. And he said to me, he's taking me to the Gestapo.

He said to me, did you write anything? Did you send an email out to Antwerp? And there was-- I think there were some papers in there that my grandmother had given me, like a, a present, a bond or something, very inexpensive thing. And my husband had sent that to my brother, just to have that.

So they had gotten that from the Gestapo, and they took me to the Gestapo. And they wanted to know if I had sent that. And I had to write, show my handwriting. And I said, no, I didn't do that. Maybe my husband did that.

So he said, where is your husband now? And I said, I don't know. He left me with my children here. That's what I gave the answer. And it was so-- I don't know how I had this idea. That was very good. And they let me go.

But the man in the car that took me to the Gestapo said to me, take all the papers, all the envelopes that you have in your house, and destroy them. Because this envelope that they caught has a certain lining inside. So they couldn't find it. They shouldn't find it. So he helped me, really. He was nice to me.

Well, it was scary. You know, I was in a room all alone with this Gestapo man, typing on the typewriter there. And-- but they let me go. I said, I don't know anything about it. My husband left me. I don't know where he is. You know, and they let me go.

INT: What happened to your parents? Were you in touch with them at that time?

DT: My parents had also gone illegally to Belgium.

INT: Oh, they left?

DT: Everybody-- my brother had left before the Kristallnacht, illegally. I was alone, really. I had no parents.

INT: Both your brothers left, also.

DT: My older brother had been-- that was a lucky incident. He had gone to Prague because he was trained for a job that would open up a business in Vienna. So he had the training in Prague, and that was just when Hitler marched into Vienna, and he never came back to Vienna. And he went from Prague right away to the United States. He got all, all the documents, and he was really lucky that he never--

INT: So how long did you stay in Vienna before--

DT: I stayed--

INT: --being able to join with your husband?

DT: I stayed in Vienna until 19-- until October. I had to leave October '39. I left to Italy, I will tell you.

INT: October '39?

DT: '39.

INT: The war started already.

DT: The war had started September 1, already, yes. I-- see, I didn't tell you that the stores were taken over by the Gestapo, right? My husband's stores. But they put money always on-- they supported me. They gave me money every month to live on that. And any expense that I had, they paid because, you know, they took all our fortunes away. .

And they-- I could even pack up all my furniture and all my belongings. That's why I have so many photos. And all my furniture, everything was packed up for me. They paid for it. And it was shipped to the United States a year ahead of the time when I came.

This was a big van. You know, they call it lift by, by--

INT: Then you knew where to ship it?

DT: It was shipped to a free load zone, standing on the harbor in a big van. You know, those, those big--

INT: You didn't ship it to any of your family who lived there?

DT: No. It was shipped to the, to the free load, because it had to be-- it has to be inspected by customs when it would be taken out, you know. When we take it out, we have to have the customs to, to--

INT: When you shipped all your things, did you know already that you're going to go to the United States?

DT: Yeah, I was hoping. Yeah. That's what we were waiting for.

INT: You were hoping, but you didn't know.

DT: We were waiting, yeah. They shipped it to the United States, and they paid for it. I packed up everything, everything. But they came to inspect it, if I have some artwork that is, that is worth, you know, valuable. Or I couldn't take out any jewelry, or anything like that, that is valuable, they didn't let me do.

But my mother put some things in at that time, even it was done before my parents left. And some-- my sister-in-law's parents put some things in. And it was standing in the free load zone for almost a year.

When my husband came-- well, that's a little later, so I want to tell you when I came, finally, to the United States. I-- everything was shipped out there. They paid for it. The food in Vienna was already rationed, was very much-- we really didn't have much to eat.

I remember my, my father-in-law-- I stayed with my father-in-law and my sister-in-law in their apartment. The Gestapo came to our house in the middle of the night, and they ransacked. There was always-- they were always harassed. In the streets they were harassed.

And my children really, they were very little, but they did suffer somehow, that Daddy wasn't there, you know? George never knew his father. He was born just before that. And when I-- I had to leave Vienna a little earlier than my boat. I finally got called to the American Embassy, and the children had a terrible rash at that time. I remember like hives.

And I was so afraid because they rejected people when they were sick, the American physician. But he was nice and he didn't pay much attention to the rash, and he gave me the visa. And I had tickets to go with the Holland American Airline, but the boats didn't go from there anymore.

INT: You don't mean airlines, you mean--

DT: A ship.

INT: Ship, yes.

DT: Sorry.

INT: You were not traveling by plane.

DT: Yeah. [LAUGHS] I was thinking of ship, but I said airline. But-- so my husband, who had gotten some money-- you know, there were people that took some money, and they gave you, for $1, maybe $0.10. And they gave the money there to him in Belgium, and he had to pay for my ship, ships.

INT: Ship line, yes.

DT: For me and the children, yeah. I had to pay for that. He did it. And I had to go over there, Italian. I had to go down to Italy. I couldn't go any more with the other air-- sh--

INT: Ships.

DT: Ship line. Ship line? Do you say ship line?

INT: The ship line, yes.

DT: [LAUGHS] And I left Vienna. Then my parents were supposed to go at the same time. My sister-in-law couldn't go because her husband was rejected by the American doctor. [SIGHS]

And my parents-in-law didn't go. I went by myself with both my children in the train. That girl that had worked for my brother-in-law that stayed with me so much, she went with me to-- on the train. I took along a potty and some, some paper-- disposable diapers.

And everybody said, what? You had disposable diapers at that time? I said, yes, what else would I have done with a child, with a few-month? And, and I took a salami along and I took a baby carriage. I had hardly any luggage with me. And I went over the Brenner Pass to Italy. She, she accompanied me up to the Brenner Pass, and I came to Italy.

And as soon as I came over the border-- this is such an interesting thing, which I'll never forget-- two men came towards me on the train to Italy. And one wore a swastika-- which was usually to identify himself so he shouldn't be bothered-- and another man, who was an Italian, who spoke only Italian. Very good-looking man.

And he wanted to know where I'm going and what's happening to me. But the man with the swastika translated it into German to me because he only spoke Italian. And when I told him that I'm going to, to Genoa, I have to wait for my boat, which goes in November.

But I had to leave Vienna because-- I had to leave early because all the documents that you had to have-- that you paid your taxes, that everything was in order-- was expiring. And if I would have to apply again for all those things, I could have never made my boat. And my husband, from Belgium, had told me, written to me, I should leave as soon as I can and not wait for any renewal of documents. I had my visa. I had my boat tickets.

So this man wanted to know where I'm going on the train. And he, he said to me, don't go to Genoa with those two little children. It is too stressful. You stop in Venice. And he took me off in Venice. He brought my children food on the train. He gave them a little pillow to sleep on. He took me off, in the middle of the night, to a hotel in Venice and paid the hotel.

And I don't know if the man was Jewish or Italian. I never know, and I always wonder what happened to him. He was-- and gave me money, a strange person. They always say there are such good happenings, too, sometimes you should always think, really. That's what I always say. He was really a strange person that just had pity to help me.

And I stayed with my children overnight in Venice, all alone, there with two kids. And it was so cold, and the woman brought me a metal hot water bottle in the bed. And I put the children, carried my luggage. He helped me with the luggage, but when I had to go back to the train, I put the children down on the Canal Grande.

Did Edith tell you that? No. On the Canal Grande in the carriage. And I ran back and forth in my luggage, to bring it to the train. And the children were standing there all alone. And when I came back, all the other Italian kids were standing around them, but nothing happened to them. They were safe.

And then I tried to feed them there. The food was so strange. The language was a little strange to me. I only understood a little. And I tried to get my husband, in Belgian, over the phone. But there were no lines. The war was on and there were no more lines. The lines were all taken. I couldn't talk to him to tell him that I'm safe in Italy.

Well, finally, I went to, to Genoa again. And I don't remember much about this time and I don't know why. I only know that my parents-in-law also came then from Vienna. They didn't have to leave so soon. At the same time we went on the same boat, TheRex.It was one of the last trips out of Italy, TheRexand TheSaturn.I know that.

INT: You mentioned Trieste before.

DT: Did I say Trieste?

INT: You meant Genoa.

DT: I meant Genoa. Genoa, yeah. I meant Genoa. I sometimes mix up a little bit. Yeah. No, that was-- I was in Venice and went to Genoa, yes, not to, not to-- he didn't want me to go to Genoa. It was too much of a ride. He wanted me to stay in--

INT: And the boat was in Genoa there?

DT: Yeah, in Genoa, TheRex.Italian line. And my parents-in-law then came from Vienna, too. They were older people, my husband's parents, and they came with me at the same time on the boat. On the boat, it was so stormy, and I was so stressed and I really broke down. That was the first time that I really broke down physically.

It was very-- I had, I had nursed and had two children in a short time, didn't have enough milk, and later on found out that there was something with my parathyroid, which produces calcium. I was really deprived of calcium and was very sick, and ended up on the boat-- I had a spasm, a muscular-- I was very sick and ended up on the boat in the hospital there.

And Edith, my daughter, was lying on the bed with me. My son didn't walk yet. And it was hard, you know, and I had to carry him all the time. Now I'm getting choked up. My Edith said, Mommy, Mommy, please don't die. In German she said it all the time, because I was really out. It wasn't a nervous break. It was a physical breakdown.

Well, when I arrived in-- the boat was so stormy. It was so-- that the sailors even were holding on. It was November. It was a very stormy 10 days. And I had a very bad berth, all the way down in deck, all the way down. You know, we had to go the stairs down. Well, it still wasn't bad.

So when I arrived in New York, my brother, who had come-- brother Max, who came from Prague to America-- picked me up on the boat. And I went to my brother-in-law, that's my husband's brother, and my parents-in-law, we went to him, to stay with him in his apartment, which was in Washington Heights, where mostly Germans settled, and they had a three-room bed, three-room apartment. And we all stayed there, I with the two children and my parents-in-law and my brother-in-law, sister-in-law, and their child. Of course, that was unbearable. It couldn't be that way.

So I had a cousin. I had a lot of family here. I had a lot of cousins, but-- American. So one of my cousins took me in, and I stayed with her for a while in Brooklyn. And she was very poor.

She was living in one of those houses. You know, housing, the public housing. She was poor. She was very nice to me. And she had two children. She had-- her husband was in institution, and she took me in and I stayed there for a while. It was a walk-up, also, five floors. I stayed with her for a while.

Then some of my family, my mother's family, they were very well off, and they tried to help me. And they wanted to have my children in a temporary home in Rockaway. It's not an orphanage. It's really a home where the parents--

INT: Like foster parents?

DT: It was not a private home. It was an institution-like, you know, like-- and they said if I will have my children there for a while, they will help me, that I should start working or make a living. I never knew when my husband will come. You know, I was without my husband. So my children--

INT: Were you worried that he wasn't in Belgium when the war--

DT: Well, he--

INT: Was Belgium occupied by then?

DT: Well, he-- wait a moment.

INT: When did you arrive in the United States?

DT: No. No. I arrived in the United States '39, November '39. No, Belgium was not occupied yet, not yet. It just happened just before, yeah. So he, he-- they wanted to help me my relatives and have these children well taken care of in that place.

And my children didn't have-- they were undernourished. They didn't have vaccinations that children in America had, so they felt that they would get all the care there and that I might be able to work for myself. But Edith, my daughter, became very-- she behaved very hysterical. She was crying. She really behaved hysterical there. She understood that she was taken away from her mother, that she didn't have a father there. She was taken away from George. George was in the--

INT: How old was she, three years old?

DT: She was just three when she arrived here. Three years old, yes. She, she was-- she was aware that she was suffering. You see, my, my, my son was a few months-- I mean, he was over a year old, so he suffered without realizing why.

And they said to me, anything that you can do, take her and stay-- take her back. She cannot stay there. She was really behaving-- she-- I think she ran away. One day, they opened up the door and she ran out.

So I, I went to somebody. The German people, the German refugees that had established themselves already in Washington Heights, they rented out rooms. So I stayed with one family there, rented a room. And I think we, we were helped by the Joint-- I think, you know, the refugees were helped. They were given some money.

INT: Joint organization?

DT: Yeah, that would help so I could pay for the rent. I had-- you know, when I left Vienna, you had to have only a few dollars on you. Not dollars, a few shilling on you. That's why the man on the train helped me to give me money for the hotel. I had no money whatsoever when I left Vienna.

And so I was helped. And I stayed in, in-- with these people, you know, that rented me that room, and Edith stayed with me. And then in January, my husband came. But the time for my arrival to January seems to me now like, like it was an eternity, and I don't know why.

But George, George stayed in that place there because he had-- he was sick in the wintertime. They took out his tonsils, and he-- and they gave him all the vaccinations. They said I should-- they said I should not take him out. I had no place yet where to stay, anyhow, when my husband came.

So he visited him, and I have pictures of him. And the child didn't know who he was at all. You know, he has such a sad look, my son, in that picture when Edith holds him. And he saw him for the first time for almost 1 and 1/2 years, saw him for the first time.

And I must say that George was [NON-ENGLISH]. But George was always such a good child, such an easy child. I mean, so, so submissive probably, in a way, because he, he just had to be good, I guess. He was a very good child, and gave me only, only nachas, like you say. I really mean it. My son really-- both my children turned out beautiful, I must say.

INT: Did it took a long time for you and your husband to learn English fluently?

DT: My husband spoke perfect English.

INT: Perfect English?

DT: He had, right away, a job, also through the family, at the World's Fair as a controller. You know, they were selling all these tents. They were selling like frankfurters and Orange Crush, and that was a big company and he worked for them. They got him this job. This family was very much involved with the movies and these type of things, so they got him this job.

He worked. We rented this apartment near the George Washington Bridge, which was a shabby house. Ugh. My children call it the dirty apartment. There were roaches and mice and bedbugs, everything in there. And we finally got that van out that we, that we had sent away from Vienna.

We had to pay money to get it out. They brought it to our street there to the apartment. They opened it up, and water came out. Everything was damaged. It was standing probably either in the rain or it was dumped into the ocean.

We had it insured. The insurance company had a big trial. They didn't know who did it, if it was the rain or if it was the sea. Everything was broken. All the-- my, my furniture fell to pieces. My bedding was-- the pictures that I have, some of them, you can see they are all damaged, you know, from the water. The books, everything. I mean, all right, everything was just falling to pieces. It was the second tragedy. But again, it was only belonging and didn't-- but it was hard on us.

We had a lawyer and the lawyer got some money and we got some money, really, for it, to just to have compensation. But I still have many of my wedding presents, like glass and crystal and silver things like this, were not damaged. So I still have that, which I am sure my children always--

INT: We're going to stop and change the tape, OK?

DT: Mm-hmm. Am I--INT: Tape four, interviewing Dora Tanner. Now you're in America. You got your, your stuff. And what is the life now?

DT: I got my husband. I got my children. But my parents--

INT: Where are they?

DT: --we had been in Antwerp. And my brother also had been in Antwerp, but he made it on time to come to the United States also, when I came about, my second brother, Morris. My parents got the visa and the war broke out, and they had to flee Belgium. They went through France through, all the flight, and, and ended up in Gurs and in Saint-Cyprian.

INT: What, what was Gurs?

DT: Gurs was a camp. It was not a concentration camp, but it was a camp for all the German refugees, because they never knew who was among them. My mother--

INT: In other words, they were-- Germans were the enemies of France and that's why they put them in those camps?

DT: Yes.

INT: Is that what it is?

DT: My, my mother suffered terrible. She told me terrible things. The rats were running around there and, and there were all the Spanish prisoners from the civil war there. It was very much suffering for her. My father was in Saint-Cyprian.

INT: Now, what was Saint-Cyprian?

DT: Also an intern--

INT: Oh, so like Gurs?

DT: Yes, an internment camp. In Gurs, there were very many famous people there, Thomas Mann and very famous people there in Gurs. And I think Mrs. Roosevelt and the HIAS helped these people. They went over the Pyrenees by foot and embarked from Lisbon, and came here, I think, in '42 somehow. So they, they really were also saved, but after a lot of suffering. And they had the American visa in Belgium already, but they went through the whole flight, and that is known what happened today.

INT: Did your parents arrive in 1942 in the United States?

DT: Yes, in the United States, and they lived in Brooklyn. My, my brother lived with me in the apartment that I had rented with my husband, where we, we had emptied out all the furniture that we had left. And he stayed with me. And I was crying every day when I heard the radio, how the people were running, and friends, how they bombarded them. And I really felt all what they had suffered. I really felt that all the time. I was crying every day. And they were--

INT: Did anybody else in your family stayed in Europe and suffered because of that?

DT: My, my mother had all her family in, in Lemberg, Lwow, Poland. My grandmother, my grandfather had died before my, my son George was named after him, Mordecai. My grandmother, who was a beautiful woman-- she even sent my husband, to Belgium, some food to help him, remember? She was an old lady.

INT: What happened to all of them?

DT: Never found out. I sometimes would like to inquire what happened, and her daughter and her son, which were married and had families. Those were all my cousins.

INT: You never heard of them?

DT: Never heard about them. Never heard about them. Didn't get anyone-- I only met once Rabbi Leiter, who is a relative of my grandma, who told me he was stayed in their house in Lemberg when the Poles, when the, you know, when the Polish were invaded. But I never knew what happened to her. And I, somehow I imagined what happened to her. She was-- of course, she was my grandmother in Poland, [INAUDIBLE] So--

INT: So how was your life in the United States from there on?

DT: In the United States, my husband-- like I said, I was lucky. I had a father who very well provided always. And I had a husband who was a good father and a good husband, and he worked hard. And I spent all the time with my children. I did some house-- Heimarbeit, you know, housework, different things, to subsidize a little bit of money.

And when the welfare ended, then we rented another apartment in-- near for Tryon Park, a nice apartment. And my brother had lived with me before and helped me pay the rent there, but then he went on his own when my parents came. And we lived there in a three-room bedroom apartment, my husband and my two children. And it was a nice life there near the park. A lot of German and Viennese people lived there, and George and Edith went to a little kindergarten there that. Was also provided by some Jewish organization. And--

INT: What, what language were your children raised on at home?

DT: Only English because--

INT: Only you spoke English to them at home?

DT: Yes. I remember, I used to go so much-- my husband came home from work and I went to the movies, and I think-- I had English in school, too. I had French as a language.

INT: But you didn't-- you know, you didn't speak German to them?

DT: No, I did not speak German, although they had their grandparents here. You see, my in-laws were there, and my parents, they don't came. But I never spoke German to them. You see, German was not a language we wanted to speak in the United States. You know, they didn't-- we were in war, you know?

INT: Now, your children, when they were raised here, were they bothered by other children because they were born foreigners or from Vienna, or they had a nice childhood?

DT: No. In Washington Heights, there were mostly German children there, German-speaking children there. Not speaking, but German families, their children were their friends mostly. They didn't-- they just started school. Edith started kindergarten in, in New York, but then we moved to Boston.

That's what I'm going to tell you. My husband got-- from the same company, he got a very good job in Boston as taking over the whole office for the New England firm there, also the same candy automats and movies. Because the boss there, Mr. [? Klibanoff ?] was inducted. He was a major in the army, and my husband took over the business as an employee. You know, he worked for them. And he had to travel a lot, too, and we moved to Boston.

Now, in Boston, my-- I remember my daughter had once came up crying to me. She was playing out with other children, non-Jewish children. And a child, one child made a very sad remark. She said, you're Jewish. You killed Christ. She really said that to my daughter. She never forgets that.

INT: How old was she when she heard that?

DT: She was maybe six years old. Yes, she had started first grade. And that was the only incident. But the schools, wonderful schools in Boston. And the teachers, they always used to say, I wish all your children, my children, would be like yours. You know, my children were well-behaved, polite, quiet, not aggressive. And the teachers were wonderful teachers. One of the teachers wrote a letter about Edith to me. And she said, one day, I will see paintings of your daughter or some writing, because she was so talented, Edith, you know.

INT: And was she right?

DT: Well, the paintings.

INT: She does paint?

DT: Yeah. Yeah. And, yes, she was always very good in school. Both my children had scholarships. And-- well, we stayed five years in Boston. I did also some home work. I made leather flowers. I worked for somebody and then I did it on my own, and then did beautiful things with flowers, and painted them.

INT: Where did you go after Boston?

DT: Pardon?

INT: You said you spent five years in Boston. Where did you go?

DT: Yeah, my husband went to--

INT: Where did you go after that?

DT: Then we went back-- when the major, when the boss came back from the army, my husband went back to New York because his brother had established himself in the textile business, in a wholesale textile business. And the whole family worked.

His father was an old man, and his brother-in-law, his sister's husband, and my husband, they all work together in, in that business, in Broadway near Canal Street. And they built up a nice business, a good business, and all the families lived on that. And I did sometimes help bookkeeping, too, there. So that, that was a good life.

We had a hard time finding an apartment when they came to Boston. We lived first in the Bronx. And it was-- my children went to school there. They had very good schools. I always said my children had the best schooling that anyone-- I remember the public school, they had even a swimming pool, indoor swimming pool there in the Bronx.

And then they went to junior high school. They all had-- they went always to special classes. They skipped. You know, they always skipped a year. Edith, my daughter, graduated her high school when she was 16.

She, she, she had an award. She had an award in junior high school for art. She won the medal for art. Then she went to music and art high school, if you know about it. And my son went to Bronx High School of Science, which is one of the best schools.

INT: What does he do now?

DT: My son is professor of physiology in medical school in Indianapolis. He is-- he was a Harvard Graduate, Phi Beta Kappa, all free and scholarships. Everything was free. He went to Cornell. He won the state's-- the New York state scholarship. Went to Cornell. Then he went to Harvard in a program for his PhD. Didn't cost us anything because they were so bright. Not because-- they were bright. He won the state scholarship.

INT: Is he married?

DT: He is married, has two lovely children. His wife also has a PhD. After the children grew up, she went back to college for a PhD. He met her at Harvard. My son met his wife at Harvard, and they got married. And he was a very quiet, always very, very introverted, young man. That's why he went into research. He was always interested.

INT: Does he live around Santa Barbara, or where, where does he live?

DT: No. My son lives in Indianapolis.

INT: In Indianapolis.

DT: He was the professor. The head of the department waited for him to come. He wanted my son to come there. He got many awards. I have some things from him that he was once, was nominated as the most liked teacher by the students and by the faculty. He was on a sabbatical in Heidelberg. They wanted him to come there.

They wrote him a letter, because he does kidney research. They wrote him a letter, that we have all the technology in Germany but we don't have the brains. He got that letter, because he was really always with honors. You know, Phi Beta Kappa and, and state scholarships. He was a very awarded young man.

INT: Did he marry a Jewish lady?

DT: Married a Jewish girl, who, whose parents are American-born. They were a little bit-- [SIGH] because he had-- they said he has a little bit of an accent, which I don't know. He never spoke any other language, but maybe acquired it from us.

INT: And what about your daughter?

DT: My daughter went to Music and Art High School, fell in love, and she was very young and married very young. She married, she was not even 18. And she was-- she went to Alabama because her boyfriend was there and she wanted to get married.

He was half Jewish. His father was Italian, who became Jewish. His mother was Jewish. But a very dysfunctional family, I must say. She helped him a lot. She was-- it was really her first love, it was probably--

INT: Does she have children?

DT: She has three children. She was married for 20 years. And the children are lovely, wonderful children, also, but she got divorced. Because he was 19-- not even, 19, not even 20-- when he married, and they both were too young when they had married.

INT: Is she remarried now?

DT: She remarried. She came, she came heartbroken to Santa Barbara to us, and there she met our physician, who asked my husband-- he was divorced, and he asked my husband if he can take her out. And I always thought that he's so young. He looks very young. So he took her out and he married her. And he had three children and she has three children. And they're married for 20 years now.

INT: When did you move in Santa Barbara?

DT: When we-- I, I had very bad rheumatoid arthritis. I still have it very bad. And I always wanted to live in a better climate. So we tried, we tried-- we went to, to Tuscon and we went to Palm Springs to have dry climate. But my brother-in-law, my husband's brother, had gone-- we were all on a trip once in Santa Barbara, and he, he met once a Jewish man in the street.

And he said, why don't you come and settle here? It's a very nice Jewish community here. So he decided to move here, my brother-in-law and his sister, with her husband. And we went Tuscon, and then-- trying out the climate. And then we said we want to visit my brother in Santa Barbara, my brother-in-law in Santa Barbara. And when we saw that, we said, my god, this is a place where we would really like to settle.

They sold their business in New York, and the parents had died in the meantime, the old parents. His father lived-- my husband's father lived-- he was 96. Very bright man, very alert. And my parents had died in New York. And so we decided to settle here, too. And that was 25 years ago. I live now 25 years ago.

INT: You lived-- you've been for the past 25 years in Santa Barbara.

DT: Yeah. We decided this is a good place. We don't drive. We never drove. So here you can walk, and it was a nice, little Jewish community. They tried to establish here a conservative synagogue, and we all belonged. But it didn't come through, and then we joined this reformed synagogue here.

INT: Is your husband still alive?

DT: My husband died 15 years ago.

INT: Of what?

DT: He, he had diabetes and he had a bad heart, and he was really-- he was the first one who died from those-- his brother, brother and sister. He was the first one. He, he was not well. He was very hardworking man. His brother had a heart attack when he was much younger in New York yet, and my husband took over a lot of the work in business. And he took, took it easy, his brother-in-law.

INT: And what do you do these days? You're retired?

DT: Yeah. Retired, yes. I, I--

INT: How do you occupy your days?

DT: Well, when I came here, I started a lot of adult education, and beautiful educational possibilities here. I went to-- had French here. You know, my husband was very good in French. We both went to the classes here. We renewed our French. He was very well.

And there was a lot to do here, a lot of music. We love music. There's the Academy of the West here, Music Academy of the West. And we always kept ourselves busy. And I took care of-- we took care of each other.

INT: Do you, do you belong to any other Jewish organization?

DT: Yes, I belonged to Hadassah. I belonged to ORT. I used to belong to Brandeis. And we were members of the temple. And-- but I went to live with my cousin, you know, who is my second cousin, when he lost his wife, his two wives. He came here, and he said if I want to live with him.

So we spend half a year in Miami and half a year here. This is the first year that he didn't come. He's 91. He would be 92 years old now. So I really feel sorry. He had lost a daughter and he had lost two wives, so I lived with him. He is-- his name is Wynd. And my grandmother, my father's mother, was a Wynd, so it's really-- we knew each other from Vienna, and the whole family's father and everyone. So--

INT: You still live with him?

DT: I still live with him. I usually go back and forth. This time-- he just went into a retirement hotel, you know. So this time I stayed longer because I had a few problems, health problems. But I, of course, was always very interested in my grandchildren, always helped them. We always helped them with college and everything, because the father couldn't do much, you know? And--

INT: Let me ask you something. Do you think that the Holocaust could happen again?

DT: My son, who just came back from, from, from Heidelberg, and I have a letter from him where he speaks with his German professor, and he tells me that the-- he writes in this letter, the older German population said they couldn't do anything. They could not help it. They couldn't prevent it. While the children of his say it is a great shame and they were responsible for it and why did it, did they let it happen?

He had this discussion with them and they just wrote it down for me. I wouldn't say that the Holocaust would-- could happen, but there is plenty of antisemitism going on right now here. We know that, all these movements that are, and it is scary. But I don't think that only the Jewish people are in danger that way. I think the whole world is in danger that way, because there's too much hate now.

INT: What would you like to say to your grandchildren?

DT: To my grandchildren, I would give them hope. I, I don't want to-- and I think that-- my grandson, my grandson, my son's son, is very, became very religious, Orthodox. He, whatever he does, you know, I told you, he, he was a presidential scholar. He had a scholarship at the University of-- he's a, he's a summa cum laude Harvard Graduate, and he had a scholarship for his MD and PhD at the University of Pennsylvania.

He's a very idealistic, young man, very, very religious, very Orthodox. His wife wears a sheitel now, and he puts on the tefillin every day. But he is the type of a person, whatever he does he does to, to perfection, really. So I'm sure he believes very much in his religion and he has hope, you know.

